<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Buyid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Buyids, Buyid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Buyid dynasty was a Shia Iranian dynasty from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a> that controlled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in the tenth and eleventh centuries. After taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 945, the Buyids ruled while keeping the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> as figureheads. They were displaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>, whose leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril Beg</a> entered Baghdad in 1055, a key step in the rise of the Seljuk Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Buyid dynasty" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ro4444 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buyids_970.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Buyids descended from Panah-Khusrow, a Zoroastrian from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a>. His son Buya, a fisherman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahijan">Lahijan</a>, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and Buya's three sons, Ali, Hasan and Ahmad, founded the dynasty. The Buyids claimed royal descent from the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-v">Bahram V</a>. Ali ibn Buya served the Daylamite warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makan-ibn-kaki">Makan ibn Kaki</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardavij">Mardavij</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyarid-dynasty">Ziyarid dynasty</a>, who granted him Karaj in 932. When Mardavij planned to have him killed, the brothers fled with 400 Daylamite followers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, won Arrajan and defeated the Abbasid general Yaqut for control of the province. Ali then took its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardavij">Mardavij</a>'s assassination in 935 threw the Ziyarid lands into chaos. Ali and Ahmad conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzistan</a>, while Hasan took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and in 943 Ray, so gaining all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a>. In 945 Ahmad entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. The Abbasid court submitted to him as the real ruler, and the Buyids built a palace just north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s, while the caliph, left with no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, became a figurehead. The brothers took the honorific titles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla">Imad al-Dawla</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rukn-al-dawla">Rukn al-Dawla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> fell in 967, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> in 967, the Jazira in 979, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> in 980 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gorgan</a> in 981.</p><p>Fanna Khusraw, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>, who ruled from 949 to 983, took the dynasty to its height. After his death his sons fought each other, local dynasties became independent in parts of the realm and the confederation slowly broke up. In 1029 Mahmud of Ghazna deposed the Buyid ruler at Ray, who had asked for help against his own Daylami troops. In 1055 the Seljuk leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and ousted the last Buyid ruler there, and the dynasty's rule ended in 1062.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Buyids formed a confederation of three principalities, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, in which land was divided among the sons and each amir enjoyed a high degree of autonomy. The senior amir had formal precedence but little control outside his own lands. Their army began with Daylamite foot soldiers and added Turks to the cavalry, and the two groups often quarrelled. Soldiers were paid by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqtas</a>, rights to a share of a province's tax revenue. The Buyids revived Sasanian titles and carved inscriptions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, but used Arabic as the language of their realm and patronised Arabic poets and historians, among them al-Mutanabbi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>'s court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>.</p><p>Initially Zaydi Shi'a, they became Twelver Shi'a, patronised Twelver scholars and built at the shrines of Kadhimiya, but they kept the Sunni Abbasid caliphs as figureheads and were known as tolerant of the Sunni majority. Their realm included many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a> and Christians. They restored the Baduriya dam, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/band-e-amir-dam">Band-e Amir</a> dam near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, a hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and a mausoleum at the burial place of Ali.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Buyid dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Military power passed increasingly to provincial commanders and then to outright successor dynasties: the Tahirids, Saffarids and Samanids in the east, and later the <b>Buyids</b> and Seljuks at the centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the west Mahmud defeated the Qarakhanids near Balkh in 1008, annexed Khwarazm in 1017 and took Rayy from the <b>Buyids</b> in 1029.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tughril entered Baghdad in 1055 and freed the Abbasid caliph Al-Qa'im from the tutelage of the Shia <b>Buyids</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From 861 Turkish soldiers murdered or deposed four caliphs in nine years, and from 936 a military commander, the amir al-umara, held real power, until the <b>Buyids</b> took Baghdad in 945 and left the caliph a figurehead.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Abbasid caliph al-Mustakfi, who had reportedly intrigued against Mu'izz al-Dawla with some of the <b>Buyids</b>' Daylamite officers, was deposed on Mu'izz al-Dawla's orders.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-29">29 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Abu'l-Fath Muhammad ibn Annaz, chief of the Kurdish Shadhanjan tribe, established himself at Hulwan on the road from Baghdad to Hamadan around 990, as <b>Buyid</b> authority weakened.</blockquote><a href="/state/annazid-dynasty">Annazid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
